# One line ending for everything, decided once here instead of per machine.
#
# Without this file Git stores whatever bytes were committed, so what lands in
# the repository depends on the editor and the operating system of whoever
# touched a file last. This repository had 97 files stored CRLF and 21 stored
# LF, and the mixture cost real time: a script that read an LF file and wrote it
# back on Windows turned every line into a changed line, which produced a diff
# of 266 lines for a change of two.
#
# `text=auto` is the part that fixes it. Git normalises text files to LF when
# they are checked in, so it no longer matters what the working copy looks like.
# `eol=lf` keeps the working copy the same as the stored form, so there is no
# conversion anywhere and no dependence on a per-developer `core.autocrlf`.
#
# LF and not CRLF, although this team edits on Windows: `eol=crlf` would force
# CRLF on every platform, including the Linux CI, and the two shell scripts
# below would fail there with `bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M`. Nothing in this
# repository needs CRLF - there is no .bat and no .cmd file.
* text=auto eol=lf
